    "result_text": "Green. Done.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI agreed with the finding \u2014 it correctly identified that my `AssignCapacityWarning` duplicated both the city scan and the `CITY_CAPACITY` constant, violating the \"count once, share via variable\" idiom the daily-registration trio already establishes. Refactored to mirror it:\n\n1. **`CountCityOwners`** (new) \u2014 does the case-insensitive `findAll()` city scan once (off the DTO's city, exactly as `EnsureCityCapacity` used to) and publishes `@Qualified(\"cityOwnerCount\") Out<Integer>`. Wired into the YAML before `ensureCityCapacity`.\n\n2. **`EnsureCityCapacity`** \u2014 no longer scans; reads `@Qualified(\"cityOwnerCount\") @Val Integer` and remains the sole owner of `CITY_CAPACITY = 50` (the hard limit).\n\n3. **`AssignCapacityWarning`** \u2014 dropped its scan, the `OwnerRepository` dependency, and the duplicate `CITY_CAPACITY`; now reads the shared `cityOwnerCount` and keeps only `CAPACITY_WARNING_THRESHOLD = 40`. Since the hard rejection at 50 has already fired upstream, the count here is always `< 50`, so `count >= 40` is sufficient.\n\nOne detail worth noting beyond the review's sketch: the repo forbids two same-typed variables in a pipeline without disambiguation, and there's already an unqualified `Integer` (`dailyRegistrationCount`) plus a qualified one (`householdSize`). So the new count uses `net.officefloor.plugin.clazz.Qualified(\"cityOwnerCount\")` on the producer and both consumers \u2014 matching the `householdSize` precedent \u2014 rather than an unqualified `Integer` that would collide.\n\nNet result: one city scan instead of two, the hard limit defined once, and the new step reads like the sibling warning steps. Full suite (`AcceptanceTest`, including `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity`) is green.",
